Z||000000||2FAST4U||12-07-1999||02:12 AM||||Does anybody know the approximate wieght of a 1985 2M4?<BR>I'm also wondering about the final drive ratio or transaxle ratio?<P>Thanks Much||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-07-1999||10:21 AM||||My '86 2m4 (auto, A/C, tilt, sunroof) weighs 2,653#.  Don't look at the door sticker.  It's probably wrong.  This is with a full tank of gas, but not me in it on 14" sawblade rims.  Seemed a little light, but it was weighed on a state (DOT) certified scale.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||12-08-1999||12:52 PM||||Gentlemen,<P>I can give you a detailed weight of vehicle either with a full tank or with 3 gallons (there are almost always 1-2 gallons that are unusable.. so read 3 gallons as 1 gallon).  Furthermore, I can add or subtract weight based on most factory options (even the various weights of different radios are listed).  If you really want to know what GM says your vehicle weighs... let me know.  I do need the year of the vehicle and the model of course.  <P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||4
Z||000003||Phil||12-08-1999||03:03 PM||||For about $6 you can go to a truck stop with scales and get an exact weight.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-09-1999||01:01 AM||||That's what I meant by a state (DOT) certified scale.  If you don't have one of these around, try a grain elevator.  Their scales are fairly accurate although not usually certified by anybody other than Bob the elevator operator <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||12-09-1999||02:50 AM||||Don't know about all states, but in Kansas the Co-op scales are state certified. I was just told a couple weeks ago that the one closest to where I work leaves the scale on and you can just drive right up anytime and look over through their window to see the weight. Next time I'm over that way with the Batmobile, I think I'll try it. I'm curious what a V8 Fiero weighs.||||reg||1
